# Line endings are a correctness issue here, not a preference.
#
# This machine has `core.autocrlf=true`, so a fresh clone on any Windows host
# would check `docker/entrypoint.sh` out with CRLF line endings. The container
# then fails with:
#
#     /app/entrypoint.sh: /bin/sh^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# That is a one-character difference between a portal that boots and a portal
# that does not, and it would only appear on a reviewer's machine — never on
# the machine that wrote the file, because the author already has it in working
# order. **A defect that can only be seen by the person we are shipping to is
# the worst kind to ship.**
#
# So: force LF in the repository, and let Git normalise on the way in so that
# contributors on any platform commit the same bytes.

* text=auto eol=lf

# The shell entrypoint and the justfile are executed, not read. A CR in either
# one is a runtime error, not a cosmetic one.
docker/entrypoint.sh   text eol=lf
justfile               text eol=lf

# Files the build tooling parses. Same reasoning: a stray CR can change a
# regex, a shebang, or a Dockerfile continuation line.
Dockerfile             text eol=lf
docker-compose.yml     text eol=lf
.dockerignore          text eol=lf
.gitignore             text eol=lf
pyproject.toml         text eol=lf
.dogfood.toml          text eol=lf
*.py                   text eol=lf
*.md                   text eol=lf
*.json                 text eol=lf
*.txt                  text eol=lf
*.yml                  text eol=lf
*.yaml                 text eol=lf
*.toml                 text eol=lf
*.html                 text eol=lf
*.css                  text eol=lf

# run.py and fixtures.json are the ORGANIZERS' files, committed byte-for-byte
# so that acceptance-report.txt is reproducible. `run.py` is executed directly
# by the panel, so its shebang and its CRLF/LF state both matter; pinning it
# here means a Windows contributor cannot silently rewrite the file we all
# score against. `-text` disables all EOL conversion, not just the default.
run.py            -text
fixtures.json     -text

# Never commit a binary as text, and never let a merge try to line-diff one.
*.sqlite3   binary
*.sqlite3-wal binary
*.sqlite3-shm binary
*.png        binary
*.jpg        binary
*.gif        binary
*.ico        binary
*.pdf        binary
*.pem        binary
*.key        binary

# Generated by `just report`, and committed whatever it says. It is a
# transcript, so it is text, but it is produced — never hand-edited.
acceptance-report.txt text eol=lf
